 "parts": [
  "Sixty-four people wore a nylon sleeve for six hours, just to find out why mosquitoes pick you.",
  "Some were far more attractive than others.",
  "And the favorites had something in common: much higher levels of fatty acids on their skin.",
  "Even stranger, those levels stayed stable for a year or more.",
  "But the researchers were careful. It's a link, not proof of cause.",
  "Next clue: bacteria.",
  "In a study of forty-eight men, the ones mosquitoes loved most had more bacteria on their skin, but fewer kinds.",
  "Then there's family. Identical twins were more alike than non-identical twins in how attractive they were to mosquitoes.",
  "It was a small pilot study, so it's a hint, not a verdict.",
  "You've heard type O gets bitten most.",
  "One lab study with tiger mosquitoes saw more landings on type O than on type A.",
  "But its own authors said blood-group markers didn't explain the preference overall.",
  "Scientists still debate it.",
  "One more. In Burkina Faso, a study found a liter of beer made people more attractive to mosquitoes.",
  "And nobody knows exactly why."
 ],
